                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_bcn_right:     `<strong>Right read on Barcelona.</strong> The exits are about rigidity and the commute — that is work-family <em>conflict</em> you can remove with flexibility. Fix the friction and turnover intention falls.`,
                    coach_r1_bcn_enrich:    `<strong>Half right.</strong> Enrichment perks won't hurt, but Barcelona's people are leaving over rigidity and commuting — that's conflict to remove first, not engagement to top up. You're treating the symptom Munich has, not the one Barcelona has.`,
                    coach_r1_bcn_pay:       `<strong>Pay is the expensive misdiagnosis.</strong> Matching salaries is a bidding war you'll lose, and exit interviews didn't cite pay — they cited rigidity and the commute. This is a flexibility problem, not a comp problem.`,
                    coach_r1_bcn_nothing:   `<strong>'A one-off' is how you lose the next eleven.</strong> Nineteen percent turnover and 38% considering leaving is a structural crisis, not noise. Hold steady and the CEO imposes the return-to-office mandate for you.`,
                    coach_r1_mun_conflict:  `<strong>Munich's problem was never conflict.</strong> Giving autonomous, client-site consultants 'the same remote rules as Barcelona' removes friction they don't feel and ignores the disengagement that does the damage. They need <em>enrichment</em>, not more remote.`,
                    coach_r1_mun_turnover:  `<strong>Munich's 9% turnover isn't the disease.</strong> Throwing retention bonuses at a low-turnover unit buys nothing — the signal is engagement at 61% against a 74% target. Spend on enrichment, not on a number that's already fine.`,
                    coach_r1_mun_nothing:   `<strong>Leaving Munich out is the one-size error in reverse.</strong> Low turnover masks disengagement; ignore it and you bank a quiet, switched-off unit. Engagement is the leading indicator here — design for it.`,
                    coach_r1_blunt:         `<strong>Your diagnosis is blunt.</strong> If you can't separate conflict-reduction from enrichment, you'll prescribe the same medicine to two different illnesses. Sharpen the distinction before you choose policies.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp two-culture diagnosis.</strong> Barcelona = remove work-family conflict; Munich = create work-family enrichment. That's Masuda's point made operational: the same arrangement lands differently by culture, so design per unit.`,
                    coach_r2_bcn_hybrid:    `<strong>Structured hybrid is the evidenced choice for Barcelona.</strong> It carries the strongest work-family-enrichment evidence and removes the commuting conflict driving exits — without the coverage chaos of the four-day week.`,
                    coach_r2_bcn_flex:      `<strong>Flextime is cheap and helps a little.</strong> It eases the rigidity, but the retention lift is modest next to structured hybrid. You've left enrichment on the table to save budget.`,
                    coach_r2_fourday:       `<strong>The four-day week tested off the charts — and that's the trap.</strong> High appeal, high collateral: your ~40% client-site and 24/7 roles can't take it, and fairness just dropped. Appeal isn't fit.`,
                    coach_r2_mun_hybrid:    `<strong>You've bolted process onto people who self-manage.</strong> Structured hybrid adds coordination overhead to autonomous Munich consultants who didn't lack flexibility — engagement slips. They need enrichment, not a calendar.`,
                    coach_r2_mun_fourday:   `<strong>A four-day week collides with client-site schedules.</strong> Munich's consultants are on client time; a compressed week creates coverage gaps and grievances without lifting engagement. Wrong tool for this culture.`,
                    coach_r2_onesize:       `<strong>One identical rollout is the classic error.</strong> Barcelona may love the hybrid, but Munich's autonomous consultants get bureaucracy they didn't need and engagement drops. Masuda: same arrangement, different outcome by culture.`,
                    coach_r2_fit:           `<strong>That's context-sensitive design.</strong> Structured hybrid for Barcelona's conflict, light flextime/outcome-autonomy for Munich's enrichment — two cultures, two fitted policies under the one cap.`,
                    coach_r2_blanket:       `<strong>A blanket eligibility rule with no 'why' breeds grievances.</strong> Telling the ~40% who can't flex 'eligible roles only' with no principle and no alternative raises turnover intention even among people who stay.`,
                    coach_r2_everyone:      `<strong>Flexibility for everyone ignores coverage reality.</strong> Client-site and 24/7 roles cannot flex the same way; promising it anyway breaks service and then breaks trust when you walk it back.`,
                    coach_r2_underinvest:   `<strong>You've under-funded manager capability.</strong> Skimp now and it comes straight back in Round 3 as managers who can't lead flexible client-site teams. Pay a little now or a lot later.`,
                    coach_r3_coach:         `<strong>Coaching + outcome-based norms is the real fix.</strong> Managers learning to lead by outcomes — not presence — is exactly what client-site flexible work needs. Munich engagement responds.`,
                    coach_r3_memo:          `<strong>A memo and an FAQ is cheap — and thin.</strong> Capability isn't a document problem; managers learn to lead flexible teams by practising, not by reading. Fine if you trained them earlier, weak if you didn't.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>'They'll adapt' is a gamble you're losing.</strong> Untrained managers default to presence-policing, which kills the enrichment you were buying. The capability gap doesn't close by itself.`,
                    coach_r3_token:         `<strong>A bonus buys quiet, not fairness.</strong> Procedural-justice research is clear: people accept 'no' when the process is just and there's an alternative. A one-off payment with no process change leaves the grievance alive.`,
                    coach_r3_dismiss:       `<strong>Dismissing the grievance is how fairness becomes turnover.</strong> 'Their roles can't flex, end of story' tells 40% of staff they're second-class — and perceived unfairness raises intention to leave even among those who stay.`,
                    coach_r3_rollback:      `<strong>Rolling back the four-day week throws out the win to fix the symptom.</strong> Scrapping the most popular benefit over one coverage complaint torches retention. Protect coverage with rotas and keep the policy intact.`,
                    coach_r3_nothing:       `<strong>'One complaint won't sink a customer' — until it does.</strong> Lost Friday coverage is a service-level breach; ignore it and you trade an HR win for a churned account. Protect the service without scrapping the benefit.`,
                    coach_r3_intact:        `<strong>Policy intact, service protected, fairness explained.</strong> Staggered rotas keep Friday covered, the principle is stated openly, and the ineligible team gets a real alternative. That's the backlash defused procedurally.`,
                    coach_r3_unprotected:   `<strong>The non-eligible roles feel like second-class citizens.</strong> Low effort to keep eligibility fair leaves the ~40% who can't flex resentful — and that resentment shows up as turnover intention. Fairness is a design choice, not an afterthought.`,
                    coach_r4_leading:       `<strong>Leading indicators are the right dashboard.</strong> Turnover intention, satisfaction, work-family conflict AND cost tell you the policy is working before exits happen — and prove it's the policy, not a slow labour market.`,
                    coach_r4_lagging:       `<strong>Lagging-only is the easy number.</strong> Quarterly turnover % and spend satisfy the CEO today but won't warn you when intention is rising again. You measured what's convenient, not what's leading.`,
                    coach_r4_cost:          `<strong>Cost-only proves the wrong thing.</strong> 'The budget held' says nothing about whether turnover fell or engagement rose. Hold the budget AND measure the outcomes, or you've optimised for the cheapest failure.`,
                    coach_r4_annual:        `<strong>An annual review is too slow for a turnover crisis.</strong> By the time you judge it at twelve months, the next walkout has happened. Check at two quarters with a trigger to adjust.`,
                    coach_r4_nocheck:       `<strong>No checkpoint is declaring victory at launch.</strong> Policies decay; without a pre-agreed checkpoint and adjustment trigger you won't catch the slide until the CEO does. Set the two-quarter trigger.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a CEO-ready, honest case.</strong> Different policies by culture, a stated fairness principle, leading indicators, and naming what you deliberately did not fund. That's how you avert the return-to-office mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Spin loses the room.</strong> Claiming turnover is 'already down' when the labour market is just slow is the exact move that invites a CEO to demand a number you can't defend. Lead with leading indicators, not optimism.`,
                    coach_r4_uniform:       `<strong>You conceded the return-to-office mandate.</strong> That's the one outcome HR most wanted to avoid — both units lose flexibility, Barcelona's exits accelerate, and the two-culture design you built is erased. The worst ending.`,

