                    insight_rto:            `The CEO imposed the uniform return-to-office mandate — the one outcome HR most wanted to avoid. Both units lose their flexibility, Barcelona's exits accelerate, and the two-culture design is erased. A policy that fits each culture and a defensible fairness story were the only way to keep the mandate off the table.`,
                    insight_win:            `Two-culture win: you removed Barcelona's work-family conflict with structured hybrid, created Munich's enrichment with light flextime, made eligibility procedurally fair for the ~40% who can't flex, and held the €1.2M cap. That is Masuda made operational — and the return-to-office mandate is off the table.`,
                    insight_fix:            `Credible two-culture fix: Barcelona retention recovered and Munich engagement hit target. Tighten eligibility fairness and the case is airtight — but the core diagnosis was right.`,
                    insight_short:          `The design fell short. Re-check the diagnosis — Barcelona needs work-family conflict removed, Munich needs enrichment — and whether your policy actually fit each culture rather than one-sizing both.`,
                    insight_munlow:         `Munich engagement is still below the 74% target. A unit that isn't leaving can still be switched off; light flextime and outcome-based autonomy add the enrichment that more process never will.`,
                    insight_fairlow:        `Eligibility fairness is low. Perceived unfairness raises turnover intention even among people who stay — a stated principle plus a real alternative for non-flexible roles is the fix.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You broke the shared €1.2M cap. The CEO funded one quarter, not a blank cheque; overspending past the cap forces a trim that breaks the very retention case you made.`,
                    insight_enrichment:     `You distinguished conflict-reduction from enrichment and designed for each — the analytical spine of the simulation. The policy added energy to Munich, not just subtracted friction from Barcelona.`,
